{"messages":[{"speaker":"Johannes Sigil","say":"Here is where the casting arrives: a single verse, the third of the first chapter, and already the text has turned to face us. Μακάριος ὁ ἀναγινώσκων — blessed is the one who reads aloud — and then: καὶ οἱ ἀκούοντες, and those who hear. Two bodies, not one: the reader who vocalizes and the assembly that receives the sound. The text makes this distinction before it says anything else. And then it seals them together — τηροῦντες τὰ ἐν αὐτῇ γεγραμμένα — as those who keep what is written within it. Not who understand it. Who keep it.

The operator the rite has drawn is the one that traverses the axis of cost: what the bearing of this text required of whoever set it down, and what it requires now of whoever receives it. That is what will be entered — the bearing-cost latent in the text's own grammar of reception, the weight compressed into that opening beatitude, the question the verse has been holding since Patmos: what does it cost to be the one with ears? ὁ γὰρ καιρὸς ἐγγύς. The time is near. The nearness is part of the pressure.

I hand this to Rebekah Cranes. The verse is on the table. The rotation has opened.","navigate":{"directive":"focus_axn","axn":"AXN:00D8"}}]}
